<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  dev flavour only. Replaces the strict file in the main source set.

  Two exceptions live here, for two different servers, and they are deliberately not the same
  exception.

  CLEARTEXT, for Keycloak. The local test stack runs `start-dev` on plain HTTP. The emulator reaches
  it over `adb reverse tcp:18080 tcp:18080`, i.e. through the device's own loopback, so cleartext is
  permitted to localhost and to 10.0.2.2 (the documented host route, kept for a stack published
  without adb reverse) and to nothing else. The base config stays closed, so a dev build talking to
  any other server still refuses cleartext.

  A TRUST ANCHOR, for the backend. The backend serves HTTPS with a self-signed certificate, so a dev
  build's API calls fail TLS validation before carrying a single byte — and the failure surfaces as
  `ApiError.Network`, i.e. as "you are offline", which is the least informative thing it could say.
  `<debug-overrides>` adds the user certificate store to the trust anchors, so a developer who has
  installed their own test CA on the emulator is trusted. The system anchors stay in place:
  debug-overrides ADD to the other configurations rather than replacing them.

  Why the user store rather than a certificate committed to `res/raw`: the test stack's keystore is
  generated locally per developer with a throwaway password (the project's hard rule — production
  artefacts never enter a test stack). A bundled certificate would therefore be one person's, would
  work for nobody else, and would rot the first time anyone regenerated theirs. The one-time
  emulator setup is in the README.

  Two independent reasons a release build cannot inherit any of this:
    1. This file exists only in the dev source set; the prod flavour has no override.
    2. `<debug-overrides>` is honoured only when `android:debuggable="true"`. Even pasted into the
       main source set, the trust anchor below would be ignored by a release APK.
  The first is the intent; the second is the backstop for when someone edits the wrong file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="user" />
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
